diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index b9b9c76..5d462bb 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -1,5 +1,36 @@
 # Changelog
 
+## 0.9.3
+
+Fixes for billing complaints. Behaviour that changes for existing callers:
+
+- Renewal dates are counted from the anchor every time, using the real length of each
+  month, so a 31st anchor goes back to the 31st after a short month (0.2.1 intended
+  this but renewals were chained from the previous date).
+- Amounts use the currency's own decimals when read, written, exported or shown on
+  statements: JPY has none, KWD has three.
+- The day of a plan change is billed at the new price, as `docs/proration.md` says. The
+  credit for the old terms is a share of what was charged for them after discount,
+  never more than is left of it, at the tax rate it was charged at. A plan change no
+  longer uses up a coupon period, and the coupon discounts the change.
+- A plan change no longer wipes a change scheduled for the next renewal.
+- Tax is worked out per rate on the sum of net amounts and then spread over the lines,
+  so lines add up to the tax total and each stays within a unit of its exact tax.
+- `allocate` splits with largest remainders: parts stay within a unit of their share,
+  zero weights get nothing, negative amounts mirror positive ones.
+- Account credit now settles an invoice like a payment: it reduces the amount due, not
+  the net, tax or revenue.
+- Credit notes are limited by what was charged after discount, count credit lines from
+  plan changes, and return tax in step with the net credited, so a fully credited
+  line returns exactly its tax.
+- A credit note goes first against what is owed, then back as cash up to the cash
+  received, and the rest to the customer's account (`settleCreditNote` takes
+  `cashAvailable`).
+- Revenue, tax reports and deferred revenue no longer mix credit notes from other
+  currencies; deferred revenue takes back credit notes.
+- Invoice numbers are only used once an invoice is built, so failed requests leave no gaps.
+- `docs/rounding.md` said the default was half-up; it is half-even, as decided in 0.4.0.
+
 ## 0.9.2
 
 - Statements list payments and write-offs.
diff --git a/docs/proration.md b/docs/proration.md
index 081fa90..98cd876 100644
--- a/docs/proration.md
+++ b/docs/proration.md
@@ -13,8 +13,26 @@ from the morning of the change date, so that day counts as time left on the peri
 change on the first day of a period therefore credits the old price in full and charges
 the new price in full.
 
-The amount is `price * days left / days in period`, rounded to a whole minor unit in the
-default rounding mode. The credit and the charge are rounded separately.
+The charge is `price * days left / days in period`, rounded to a whole minor unit in the
+default rounding mode (half-even, see [Rounding](rounding.md)).
+
+## What is given back
+
+The credit is worked out from what the customer was actually charged for the stretch
+they are leaving, after any discount, not from the list price. It is that charge times
+the days left in the stretch divided by the days in the stretch. The stretch is the
+whole period if there has been no change yet, or the time since the last change if
+there has. So a customer on a discount gets back a share of the discounted amount they
+paid, and a second change in the same period gives back a share of what the first
+change charged.
+
+The credit is never more than what is left of that charge once earlier credit notes and
+credits have been taken off. It goes back at the tax rate it was charged at, even if
+the rate has changed since. The new charge uses the rate in force on the change date.
+
+A coupon covers everything billed inside the periods it runs for, so a plan change in a
+covered period is discounted too. A plan change does not use up one of the coupon's
+periods.
 
 ## Worked example
 
diff --git a/docs/rounding.md b/docs/rounding.md
index 7a1b2bf..4edefcd 100644
--- a/docs/rounding.md
+++ b/docs/rounding.md
@@ -5,8 +5,11 @@ use exact integer arithmetic and then round once.
 
 ## The default mode
 
-The default rounding mode is half-up: an exact half always rounds away from zero.
-So 2.5 rounds to 3, 3.5 rounds to 4 and -2.5 rounds to -3.
+The default rounding mode is half-even (banker's rounding): an exact half rounds to
+the nearest even whole number. So 2.5 rounds to 2, 3.5 rounds to 4 and -2.5 rounds
+to -2. Anything that is not exactly a half rounds to the nearest whole number as
+usual. Finance asked for this in 0.4.0 so that rounding does not lean upwards over
+many invoices.
 
 This is the mode used for tax, discounts and proration.
 
@@ -16,11 +19,22 @@ Callers can pass a mode explicitly:
 
 | Mode | 2.5 | 3.5 | 2.4 | -2.5 |
 |---|---|---|---|---|
+| `half-even` (default) | 2 | 4 | 2 | -2 |
 | `half-up` | 3 | 4 | 2 | -3 |
 | `down` | 2 | 3 | 2 | -2 |
 
+`half-up` rounds an exact half away from zero. `down` drops the fraction.
+
 ## Sign
 
 Rounding is symmetric. The result for a negative value is always the negative of
 the result for the same positive value, so a credit rounds the same way as the
 charge it reverses.
+
+## Splitting an amount
+
+When an amount has to be split across lines (a discount, or tax on an invoice), each
+part is its exact share rounded down and the units left over go one each to the
+lines with the largest remainders, the earlier line first on a tie. The parts add up
+to the amount, each is within one minor unit of its exact share, a line with no
+share gets nothing, and a negative amount splits as the mirror of the positive one.
